Description: pvr: do not wait for the previous submit on the CPU
 pvr_driver_queue_submit() started with pvr_clear_last_submits_syncs(), which
 waited on the CPU (vk_sync_wait_many() with UINT64_MAX) until every job of
 the previous vkQueueSubmit had completed and then dropped all per-queue
 syncs. The pvr DRM winsys has no threaded submit, so this wait runs in the
 submitting thread and the next submission only reaches the kernel once the
 GPU is idle. Under zink this is the flush thread, which then waits in
 DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_WAIT once per frame. The wait was added when the queue moved
 to the common vk_queue code (upstream 80f864cd23b), replacing the earlier
 tracking of the previous submit's syncs.
 .
 Keep last_job_signal_sync[] across submits instead. Jobs of one type run in
 submission order on their firmware context, so the last signal sync of a
 type covers all earlier jobs of that type, also those of earlier submits.
 Pipeline barriers and event waits (pvr_process_event_cmd_barrier()) and
 the semaphore and fence signals (pvr_process_queue_signals()) already wait
 on these syncs, so they now also order against work of earlier submits that
 is still running, instead of relying on the CPU wait. Command buffers start
 with all barriers armed (barriers_needed set to all stages in
 vkBeginCommandBuffer), so a barrier at the start of a command buffer
 that vkCmdPipelineBarrier2 records as a barrier event is resolved against
 the jobs of the previous submit. Barriers from a stage to the same single
 stage and from geometry to fragment are not recorded as an event; they rely
 on the order of the jobs on their firmware context, as within a submit.
 .
 The next-job wait syncs (next_job_wait_sync[]) are kept across submits as
 well. Besides the queue waits they carry the barriers recorded at the end
 of a command buffer, and the first job of a stage in the next submit has to
 wait for them; without the CPU wait, dropping them at the start of a submit
 let such a job overtake the work the barrier ordered it after (seen as a
 query copy under zink reading an unfinished result). Queue waits are added
 to an existing next-job wait sync instead of replacing it.
 .
 Only for this downstream stack; not intended for upstream submission.
